  clock: 1  # ddf8
  nonce: ddf8  # ddf8
  data:  # ddf8
    kind: invariant  # ddf8
    title: "The uncatalogued-module disclosure reports first-party code as unexamined third-party: rust 'crate::'/'super::'/own-crate-name and every javascript relative specifier, ~3 of 4 entries are noise"  # ddf8
    status: violated  # ddf8
    priority: 1  # ddf8
    parent:  # ddf8
    tags:  # ddf8
    - io_boundary  # ddf8
    - verify_claims  # ddf8
    - false_inconclusive  # ddf8
    - analysis_quality  # ddf8
    isbefore: []  # ddf8
    duplicate_of: []  # ddf8
    not_duplicate_of: []  # ddf8
    pr_ref:  # ddf8
    description: "MEASURED 2026-08-24 on two unmodified upstream repos, by recomputing\n_uncatalogued_external_modules over each run's own survey.\n\n  express (javascript)  17 modules reported \"the I/O catalog could not classify\"\n      FIRST-PARTY (7): ..  ../  ../../db  ./post  ./site  ./user  lib/utils\n      node stdlib (4): assert  node:http  node:path  node:querystring\n      globals (2)    : document  process\n      GENUINE (4)    : body-parser  mime-types  proxy-addr  statuses\n\n  bellman (rust)        23 modules reported\n      FIRST-PARTY (6): bellman.VerificationError (the crate's OWN name),\n                       crate.SynthesisError, crate.gadgets.boolean.Boolean,\n                       super.SynthesisError, super.boolean.Boolean,\n                       super.uint32.UInt32\n      DUAL SPELLING of one module, counted TWICE (4 spurious):\n                       bls12_381.Scalar / bls12_381::Scalar\n                       std.io / std::io ; std.cmp.Ordering / std::cmp\n                       std.sync / std::sync::Arc\n      stdlib (~4)    : std  std::iter  std::num  ...\n      GENUINE (5)    : blake2s_simd::Params  ff.Field  rand\n                       rand_xorshift::XorShiftRng  rayon\n\nSo roughly 3 of every 4 entries in the disclosure are NOT what the disclosure\nis for. `crate::` and `super::` are Rust's own SELF-REFERENCE KEYWORDS; a\njavascript specifier beginning with `.` is a path relative to the importing\nfile. Neither can be a third-party module by construction.\n\nMECHANISM, read in code. _analyzed_modules derives first-party names from the\nSRC SIDE FILE PATHS -- on bellman it yields `groth16.src.generator`,\n`groth16.src.lib`, `benches.slow`. The callee side names things by IMPORT name\n(`bellman::...`) or by keyword (`crate::`, `super::`) or by relative path\n(`../post`). _is_analyzed_module's component-bounded infix rule cannot bridge\nany of those: a crate name lives in Cargo.toml and not in the directory\nlayout, a keyword is not a name at all, and a relative specifier is meaningless\nwithout resolving it against the importing file. Verified:\n_is_analyzed_module('bellman.VerificationError', analyzed) -> False, same for\ncrate.* and super.*.\n\nTHIS IS THE THIRD FORM OF A DEFECT ALREADY FIXED TWICE. INV-fibis's 2026-08-09\nentry records the first draft of this gate \"blamed poetry's own modules -- 120\nof the remaining 171\", fixed by deriving first-party from the src side; INV-liloh\nrecords the exact-membership-vs-suffix bug in the same predicate. The\npath-derived rule solved python and does not generalise to a language whose\ncallee slot carries a keyword or a relative path.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN THE COUNT SUGGESTS: `qualifying_only` is `not\nunknown`, so ONE noise entry is enough to withhold a verdict. On these two\nrepos the first-party entries ALONE are sufficient to withhold every claim.\n\nTHE FIX IS PER-LANGUAGE AND NEEDS NO HEURISTIC. Rust: `crate::`, `super::`,\n`self::` are first-party by definition, and the package name in Cargo.toml is\nthe crate's own name. Javascript/typescript: a specifier starting with `.` or\n`/` is a relative path, i.e. first-party by definition. Both are exact rules,\nnot guesses.\n\nHONEST LIMIT ON THE PAYOFF, measured rather than implied: removing the\nfirst-party entries does NOT flip either repo's verdict to clean. express still\nreports 10 modules (node stdlib + genuine third party) and bellman still\nreports stdlib, so both stay `inconclusive`. What this buys is a disclosure a\nreader can act on instead of one that is three-quarters noise. Verdict\nAVAILABILITY is a different problem (WI-lutuh: 14 of 15 catalogued languages\ndeclare zero module_completeness).\n\nNOT MEASURED: how many other languages carry a keyword or relative-path callee\nslot (only rust and javascript were run); whether typescript behaves as\njavascript here; the first-party share on a larger repo sample."  # ddf8
    fields:  # ddf8
      statement: "The uncatalogued-module gate must report only modules whose I/O the analysis genuinely could not examine. A callee the language itself marks as first-party -- rust's crate::/super::/self:: keywords or its own package name, a javascript specifier beginning with . or / -- is source this analysis read, and reporting it as an unexamined third-party module both withholds the verdict for a false reason and buries the entries that are real."  # ddf8
      root_cause: "_analyzed_modules derives first-party names from SRC-SIDE FILE PATHS (bellman yields groth16.src.generator, groth16.src.lib) while the callee slot carries an IMPORT name (bellman::...), a SELF-REFERENCE KEYWORD (crate::, super::) or a RELATIVE PATH (../post). _is_analyzed_module's component-bounded infix rule cannot bridge any of the three: a crate name lives in Cargo.toml not in the directory layout, a keyword is not a name, and a relative specifier is meaningless until resolved against the importing file. The path-derived rule was written for python (INV-fibis 2026-08-09, INV-liloh) and does not generalise."  # ddf8
